 "inventory_source": "(FP64 vectors of 8 B per DOF, FP32 vectors of 4 B per DOF; source lines in the authors' code):\n  reference_pcg64_e29      krylov.pcg64 + CastPrecond (krylov.py:60-118, 334-346), as E29 R2 runs it\n                           (E29_review.py:155-157): x, r, p, zq; FP32 r32, z32.  The updates are fused kernels\n                           (_axpy, _xpby) and load.residual works in place, so there is no full-size temporary.\n  reference_pcg64_t21      the same plus a stored previous solution for the tightening check and an independent\n                           residual vector (planned additions of T21/T33)\n  pcg_energy_e29           E29_review.py:122-142 beside the kept reference solution: reference x, xc, r, z, q, q64,\n                           p (z.copy()), and the temporary of 'x += a_ * p' (line 137; 'r -= a_ * q' reuses its block\n                           after it is freed); FP32 r32, z32 of CastPrecond\n  pcg_energy_t20op         the same on a T20 FreeOperator, which adds its masked input copy (FP64) and its free-DOF\n                           mask (1 B per DOF)\n  fp32_control_common      E29_review.py:175-196 with E8_precision_floor.py:115-170 (pcg32, mode mg32_x64): FP64 kept\n                           reference x, ws x64, ws r64, tmp and the two simultaneous FP64 casts of b32 in the norm at\n                           E8:123 (later the uploaded iterate u takes their place); FP32 b32 (E29:175), ws x32, r, p, zq\n  fp32_control_own         the same with the operator's own V-cycle built while the common one is live (E29:199-200):\n                           a second V-cycle build peak\n  minres_library(T20 op)   cupyx minres: 12 FP64 vectors measured above the fixed parts, plus the right-hand side,\n                           the kept reference solution and one residual check vector\n  gmres_library(m)         cupyx gmres(restart m): m + 9 measured, plus the same three; small arrays counted from\n                           the source (_iterative.py:150-160, 180-184; _make_compute_hu): the FP64 Hessenberg matrix H\n                           of (m + 1) x m, the projection h (at most m) and the least-squares solution y (m).  CuPy\n                           caps the restart at the system dimension n (restart = min(restart, n)), so the count holds\n                           for m <= n only.  Measured at m = 10 and 20.\n  lobpcg_library(b)        cupyx lobpcg on (A + A^T)/2 with b block vectors: 15 b measured (the forward and transposed\n                           outputs of the symmetric-part action are allocated inside the measured solver); small arrays\n                           bounded from the source (_lobpcg.py:11-62, 165-180, 330-560): the Gram blocks, the 3b x 3b\n                           matrices gramA and gramB, the Cholesky factor, two inverses, the transformed matrix, the\n                           eigenvectors and the dense eigensolver's workspace, at most 32 (3b)^2 FP64 entries live at\n                           once, plus two b-entry history arrays per iteration (lambdaHistory, residualNormsHistory,\n                           kept to the end; each rounded up to the 512 B pool unit) for at most LOBPCG_MAXITER\n                           iterations.  The count holds on the iterative path only: when 5 b > n CuPy switches to a\n                           dense eigensolver of the n x n matrix (_lobpcg.py:292-305), which this count does not\n                           describe.  Measured at b = 3 and 6.\n  eigensolver_inplace(b)   PLANNED, NOT MEASURED: an in-place block eigensolver of 10 b + 3 vectors (X, SX, W, SW, P,\n                           SP and four b-blocks for the Rayleigh-Ritz update; forward and transposed outputs and the\n                           operator's masked copy, which is therefore not added again) plus 2 vectors for elementwise\n                           temporaries, and the same small-array bound as lobpcg_library; it is not an upper bound\n                           until the implementation exists and is measured",
 "notes": [
  "The transposed actions of the T20 operators are matrix-free: they store no transposed matrix; their memory is one FP64 output vector, inside the counts of the solves that use it.",
  "The k = 128 values of the V-cycle and of the operators are measured, not extrapolated.",
  "Pool estimates add the V-cycle's build peak, which the pool keeps cached, to the solve's arrays.",
  "Every inventory of an existing implementation is at or above the measured peak pool bytes of that implementation at k = 64 and 128 (inventory_checks).",
  "The library coefficients are measured peak used bytes above the fixed parts at k = 32 and 64; the same coefficient at both sizes, with an excess of at most 27,536 B for small arrays. The small arrays are counted from the CuPy source beside a 1 MiB allowance: GMRES's (m + 1) x m Hessenberg matrix and two m-vectors; LOBPCG's dense Rayleigh-Ritz arrays, at most 32 (3b)^2 FP64 entries, and its two per-iteration history arrays for at most 2000 iterations.",
  "Largest restart and block: each holds only on the solver path the count describes (GMRES m <= n; LOBPCG 5 b <= n, above which CuPy switches to a dense n x n eigensolver); limited_by_solver_path marks a value set by that limit, not by memory. The coefficients were measured at GMRES m = 10, 20 and LOBPCG b = 3, 6; a largest value outside them, above the largest or below the smallest measured parameter, is an extrapolation by the source count (relation_to_measured_range says which), and the job that uses it measures its workspace before admission. *_within_measured_range gives the largest fitting value between the smallest and the largest measured parameter, and null when the largest fitting value lies below the smallest measured parameter.",
  "eigensolver_inplace is a planned count for an implementation not yet written: not a bound. Its 10 b + 3 vectors include the T20 operator's masked input copy, which is not added again.",
  "The free memory at a later launch differs; each job is admitted again with its own estimate."
 ],
